<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================================
    PHPUnit Configuration — MASES Core Platform Foundation API
    ============================================================================

    Target   : PHPUnit 11.5 / Laravel 12 / PHP 8.3
    Location : <project-root>/phpunit.xml

    ============================================================================
    REVISION HISTORY
    ============================================================================

    v1.0.0 — Initial Laravel default minimal phpunit.xml (Unit + Feature
             suites only; narrow php env block).

    v1.1.0 — CR-2 expanded. Extended MASES test harness. Rejected by the
             XML parser: a comment contained two consecutive ASCII
             hyphens, which XML 1.0 forbids inside comments.

    v1.1.1 — Syntax-only correction. Comment dividers use equals-sign
             runs; literal CLI flag references rephrased to prose.
             Parsed successfully but aborted at suite bootstrap because
             it declared a suite pointing at tests/Integration, which
             does not exist. PHPUnit 11 hard-aborts on a missing suite
             directory.

    v1.1.2 — Removed the Integration and MASES suite declarations. All
             other extended configuration preserved verbatim.

    v1.1.3 — D-13 follow-up. Added the Domain suite pointing at
             mases_core/src/Domains. Disabled the display-details family
             so verbose per-item lists no longer dominate the output.
             Rejected by the XML parser at deploy time: a reworded
             comment still embedded the forbidden sequence.

    v1.1.4 — Two corrections: (a) parse fix for comment text; (b) log
             noise suppression via the null log driver. Rejected at
             deploy time: one comment still contained a literal CLI flag
             spelling with two consecutive hyphens.

    v1.1.4a — Same-revision correction. The literal CLI flag invocation
              inside the LOGGING comment is reworded to prose. This is
              the only place in the file that still contained the
              forbidden sequence. No functional XML attribute changes.

              After this correction: the file parses cleanly, the Domain
              suite is discovered, application logs are silenced, and
              the display-details family remains off.

    v1.2.0 — Noise-suppression revision. Goal: the console report must
             contain only PASS blocks and FAIL blocks. Warning and
             deprecation output of every origin was suppressed, and no
             warning or deprecation category could escalate the exit
             code. Specifically:

               (a) failOnWarning and failOnDeprecation were turned off,
                   so PHP-triggered warnings and deprecations could not
                   turn a green run red.

               (b) failOnPhpunitDeprecation and failOnPhpunitWarning
                   were declared explicitly as off, so PHPUnit's own
                   internal notices (including the metadata-in-doc-
                   comment notice emitted at collection time) could not
                   escalate the exit code either.

               (c) displayDetailsOnPhpunitDeprecations and
                   displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerWarnings remained
                   off, so neither the per-test WARN lines nor the
                   deprecation detail block were printed.

               (d) failOnRisky, failOnEmptyTestSuite,
                   beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests,
                   beStrictAboutChangesToGlobalState, and
                   beStrictAboutTestsThatDoNotTestAnything remained on.

    v1.2.1 — B1 audit remediation. Narrowed v1.2.0's noise-suppression
             posture to the part that is structurally required, and
             restored the exit-code escalation for the categories that
             surface real defects.

             RATIONALE
             ====================
             v1.2.0 was applied to silence two genuinely different
             categories of console output, and it silenced them with the
             same lever:

               1. PHPUnit's own metadata-in-doc-comment notice, emitted
                  at collection time. This notice is cosmetic, and no
                  XML attribute suppresses its EMISSION — only its
                  exit-code escalation and its display. The durable fix
                  is the Rector migration (PHP 8 attributes), which was
                  applied alongside v1.2.0 and has already removed the
                  notice at source. The two `failOnPhpunit*` flags exist
                  only as a safety net for future regressions of that
                  specific class.

               2. PHP-triggered warnings and deprecations, emitted at
                  runtime. These are NOT cosmetic. They surface real
                  defects — including silent catch-and-log listeners
                  whose signatures have drifted from their callee (see
                  R-21, `AuditWalletEvent`'s call to a non-existent
                  named parameter on `AuditService::log()`). v1.2.0's
                  `failOnWarning="false"` removed the exit-code signal
                  that would have kept such a defect visible after its
                  initial discovery.

             The two categories were conflated. v1.2.1 separates them.

             CHANGES
             ====================
               (a) failOnWarning and failOnDeprecation are restored to
                   "true". A warning or deprecation of PHP origin again
                   escalates the exit code. Real defects of the R-21
                   class surface on the next run.

               (b) failOnPhpunitWarning and failOnPhpunitDeprecation
                   remain "false". These are the two categories the
                   Rector migration was specifically intended to
                   eliminate; keeping them false is the safety net
                   v1.2.0 described, and is not a suppression of any
                   PHP-origin signal.

               (c) The display-details family remains off. This is
                   presentation only, not exit-code behaviour — the
                   exit code is the load-bearing signal, not the
                   printed list.

               (d) failOnNotice remains "false". Its pre-v1.2.0 value
                   is not recoverable from the available evidence, and
                   no incident attributable to a suppressed notice has
                   been identified. If a future audit finds one, this
                   flag is the next candidate for restoration.

             WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE
             ====================
             · All test suites, coverage sources, and env vars.
             · The LOG_CHANNEL=null setting (application-log
               suppression is orthogonal to PHPUnit's own exit-code
               flags; v1.2.0's §"LOGGING" rationale still applies).
             · beStrictAbout* and failOnRisky/failOnEmptyTestSuite.

    ============================================================================
    PURPOSE
    ============================================================================

    Defines the test suites, coverage sources, bootstrap sequence, and
    environment variables used by PHPUnit when running:

        php artisan test          (Laravel wrapper, preferred)
        vendor/bin/phpunit        (direct)

    DESIGN PRINCIPLES
    =================

    1. Hermetic tests. The suite must NOT touch the real database, mail
       transport, cache backend, or queue. Every external dependency is
       overridden in the php env block below with an in-memory, no-op,
       or null driver.

    2. Signal over noise, but signal is preserved. Cosmetic
       collection-phase notices are suppressed at source (via the
       companion Rector migration) rather than at the exit code.
       Runtime warnings and deprecations — which can indicate real
       defects — DO escalate the exit code (failOnWarning and
       failOnDeprecation are "true"). Strict-mode flags that catch real
       defects (risky tests, empty suites, stray output, global-state
       mutation) remain enabled.

    3. Quiet console. Application logging is discarded via the null log
       driver. Only PHPUnit's own reporter appears on the console.

    4. Coverage includes both application code and the MASES core.
       Coverage thresholds are enforced via the coverage-clover option
       in CI, not here.

    5. Random execution order is NOT enabled by default. Developers may
       opt in locally using PHPUnit's order-by option on the command
       line.

    USAGE
    =====

        composer test             clear config cache, then run full suite
        vendor/bin/phpunit        run all suites

    To run a single test suite, pass the suite name using PHPUnit's
    testsuite option. Suite names currently defined here are:

        Unit       tests/Unit              app-level unit tests
        Feature    tests/Feature           app-level HTTP and API tests
        Domain     mases_core/src/Domains  all domain-scoped tests,
                                           discovered recursively under
                                           any domain's Tests subtree

    To run a single test class, pass the class name using PHPUnit's
    filter option. Coverage output requires Xdebug or PCOV to be enabled
    in the CLI PHP binary.

    CI NOTES
    ========

    In CI, run PHPUnit with the coverage-clover and log-junit options
    pointed at files under build/, then parse the Clover report with
    your coverage reporter (Codecov, Coveralls, SonarQube). Do NOT rely
    on the coverage element below for threshold enforcement. That
    element exists only to declare which code is measured.
    ============================================================================
-->
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="vendor/autoload.php"
         colors="true"

         cacheDirectory=".phpunit.cache"

         executionOrder="depends,defects"
         resolveDependencies="true"
         reverseDefectList="false"

         beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="true"
         beStrictAboutChangesToGlobalState="true"
         beStrictAboutCoverageMetadata="true"
         beStrictAboutTestsThatDoNotTestAnything="true"

         failOnEmptyTestSuite="true"
         failOnRisky="true"

         failOnWarning="true"
         failOnDeprecation="true"
         failOnNotice="false"

         failOnPhpunitWarning="false"
         failOnPhpunitDeprecation="false"

         displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerDeprecations="false"
         displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerErrors="false"
         displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerNotices="false"
         displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerWarnings="false"
         displayDetailsOnPhpunitDeprecations="false"
         displayDetailsOnIncompleteTests="false"
         displayDetailsOnSkippedTests="false"

         requireCoverageMetadata="false"
>
    <!--
        ========================================================================
        TEST SUITES
        ========================================================================

        Unit        app-level pure PHP tests under tests/Unit.
                    Fast. Prefer these when possible.

        Feature     app-level HTTP and API tests under tests/Feature.
                    Boots the full kernel.

        Domain      every domain-scoped test under mases_core/src/Domains,
                    discovered recursively. Covers both per-domain Unit
                    and Feature subtrees in one sweep, so a domain author
                    can add Tests/Unit or Tests/Feature test files
                    without touching this file.

        Every PHP file under the listed directories whose name ends in
        Test.php is collected. Nested folders are walked recursively.
        Ordering is deterministic and matches directory listing order.

        WHY A SINGLE Domain SUITE RATHER THAN PER-DOMAIN SUITES
        =======================================================

        PHPUnit 11 validates every declared suite directory at bootstrap
        and aborts the entire run with exit code 2 if any one of them is
        missing. A per-domain declaration (one directory entry per
        domain) would abort the whole run if any single domain lacked a
        Tests/Unit or Tests/Feature subdirectory.

        A single directory entry pointing at mases_core/src/Domains
        avoids that class of failure entirely. PHPUnit validates the
        top-level directory (which exists) and walks its contents at
        discovery time; missing intermediates simply contribute no
        tests.

        If finer-grained control is needed later (for example, to run
        only the Wallet domain tests via a per-domain suite name),
        additional suites can be added below the Domain suite. That is
        an opt-in addition, not required for correctness.
        ========================================================================
    -->
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="Unit">
            <directory suffix="Test.php">tests/Unit</directory>
        </testsuite>

        <testsuite name="Feature">
            <directory suffix="Test.php">tests/Feature</directory>
        </testsuite>

        <testsuite name="Domain">
            <directory suffix="Test.php">mases_core/src/Domains</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>

    <!--
        ========================================================================
        COVERAGE SOURCE
        ========================================================================

        Which files are considered code under test when generating
        coverage reports. Anything outside this list is ignored:
        vendor/, tests/, config/, database/, and so on.

        Both application code (app/) and MASES core (mases_core/src/) are
        included, because a regression in either is a regression in the
        API.

        The exclude entries remove:

          · Laravel service providers (they only wire the container; not
            meaningfully testable in isolation)
          · Console Kernel (same reason)
          · Exception handler (mostly framework overrides)
          · Migration and seeder files (data, not logic)
          · Helpers.php (function bag; covered transitively)

        Adjust the excludes if the application grows a provider that
        contains real logic.
        ========================================================================
    -->
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory suffix=".php">app</directory>
            <directory suffix=".php">mases_core/src</directory>
        </include>
        <exclude>
            <directory>app/Providers</directory>
            <file>app/Console/Kernel.php</file>
            <file>app/Exceptions/Handler.php</file>
            <file>mases_core/src/Support/Helpers.php</file>
        </exclude>
    </source>

    <!--
        ========================================================================
        PHP ENVIRONMENT
        ========================================================================

        Every entry below is applied to $_ENV, $_SERVER, and putenv()
        before the framework boots. Values here OVERRIDE anything set in
        .env and .env.testing.

        These settings make the test suite hermetic: it never touches
        the real database, never sends real mail, never writes to the
        real cache backend, and never processes real queue jobs. If a
        test needs a specific override, use the application config or
        putenv inside the test itself, not here.
        ========================================================================
    -->
    <php>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            CORE APPLICATION ENVIRONMENT
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="APP_ENV"                value="testing"/>
        <env name="APP_DEBUG"              value="true"/>
        <env name="APP_KEY"                value="base64:AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA="/>
        <env name="APP_MAINTENANCE_DRIVER" value="file"/>
        <env name="APP_URL"                value="http://api.maalearna.test"/>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            AUTHENTICATION
            ================================================================

            AUTH_MODEL binds the auth pipeline (password broker,
            verification, password reset) to the MASES Core User model.
            Without this, the framework resolves App\Models\User, which
            does not satisfy the type contracts enforced by
            AuthenticationService.

            This is the change originally recorded as CR-2.
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="AUTH_MODEL"             value="MaaleArna\MASES\Core\Auth\Models\User"/>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            MASES CORE OVERRIDES
            ================================================================

            These feed the mases_*() helpers from
            mases_core/src/Support/Helpers.php
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="MASES_ENV"                    value="testing"/>
        <env name="MASES_TENANT_ISOLATION"       value="false"/>
        <env name="MASES_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID"      value=""/>
        <env name="MASES_DEFAULT_LOCALE"         value="am"/>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            DATABASE
            ================================================================

            SQLite in-memory is the fastest hermetic option. Do NOT
            point this at MySQL: parallel test runs and CI containers
            cannot share a database, and the in-memory driver is
            substantially faster.

            If a test genuinely needs MySQL-specific behaviour (JSON
            columns, full-text search, window functions), override it in
            that test's setUp via RefreshDatabase against a dedicated
            test connection.
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="DB_CONNECTION"          value="sqlite"/>
        <env name="DB_DATABASE"            value=":memory:"/>
        <env name="DB_FOREIGN_KEYS"        value="true"/>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            CACHE / SESSION / QUEUE
            ================================================================

            Array = in-process, no persistence, wiped per test.
            Sync  = jobs execute inline, deterministically.
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="CACHE_STORE"            value="array"/>
        <env name="SESSION_DRIVER"         value="array"/>
        <env name="QUEUE_CONNECTION"       value="sync"/>
        <env name="BROADCAST_CONNECTION"   value="log"/>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            MAIL
            ================================================================

            Never send mail in tests. The array mailer collects messages
            in memory and exposes them via Mail::fake() assertions.
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="MAIL_MAILER"            value="array"/>
        <env name="MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS"      value="testing@maalearna.test"/>
        <env name="MAIL_FROM_NAME"         value="MASES Test"/>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            SECURITY TUNING
            ================================================================

            Bcrypt at 4 rounds (minimum) keeps password-hashing tests
            fast without changing behaviour. Sanctum's token TTL and
            cookie settings are relaxed so tests do not depend on
            wall-clock time.
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="BCRYPT_ROUNDS"            value="4"/>
        <env name="SANCTUM_STATEFUL_DOMAINS" value="localhost,127.0.0.1,api.maalearna.test"/>
        <env name="SANCTUM_TOKEN_PREFIX"     value=""/>
        <env name="SESSION_LIFETIME"         value="120"/>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            TELEMETRY
            ================================================================

            Laravel Pulse and Telescope are disabled for tests. They
            collect telemetry that would pollute memory and slow the
            suite.
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="PULSE_ENABLED"          value="false"/>
        <env name="TELESCOPE_ENABLED"      value="false"/>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            LOGGING
            ================================================================

            The null log driver discards every log write. During tests,
            the application's own diagnostic logging is not needed: test
            assertions are the source of truth for pass or fail, and the
            failure block contains everything needed to diagnose a
            failure.

            Why this matters: many MASES tests deliberately exercise
            error paths (tenant rejection, permission denial, invalid
            credentials, and so on). Each such test causes the middleware
            to call Log::error() or Log::warning(). With the stack driver
            pointed at stderr, every one of those calls printed to the
            console and drowned PHPUnit's own output.

            To temporarily restore application logging during a debugging
            session, override the log channel at the shell to the single
            driver. That writes to storage/logs/laravel.log instead of
            the console. Alternatively, restore the stack driver pointed
            at stderr to reinstate the previous behaviour.
            ================================================================
        -->
        <env name="LOG_CHANNEL"            value="null"/>

    </php>

    <!--
        ========================================================================
        EXTENSIONS
        ========================================================================

        PHPUnit extensions that hook into the test lifecycle. Laravel 12
        registers its own bootstrap through the Composer autoloader via
        the Tests\CreatesApplication trait, so no extension is strictly
        required here.

        If a package that needs a PHPUnit extension is added later (for
        example Infection for mutation testing, or PHPUnit's built-in
        TestDox reporter), register it inside this block.

        Left empty for now: adding unnecessary extensions slows
        bootstrap.
        ========================================================================
    -->
    <extensions/>
</phpunit>